.TH PLY 1 "2026-08-01" "ply 0.1.0" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
ply \- work a goal with a toolbox until a check says it is done
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ply
.RI [ flags ]
.I goal
.br
.B ply tools
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B ply system
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ goal ]
.br
.B ply version
.br
.B ply help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ply
is an agent loop built as a filter. It sends a goal to a language model,
runs the shell commands the model writes, hands back what they printed, and
repeats until the model stops \(em then runs a check, and hands its failure
back if it failed. The answer is standard output, the typescript is
standard error, and the exit status says what happened.
.PP
It holds no provider code, no credentials, no session format and no
catalogue. When it needs a model it runs
.BR ask (1);
when it needs a procedure it runs
.BR brief (1);
and a tool is a program, so the toolbox is
.BR PATH .
.SS The protocol
One model turn is either one action or one report. An action is optional
leading prose followed by exactly one nonempty fenced shell block as the
turn's final content:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\(ga\(ga\(gaply
go test ./... 2>&1 | tail \-20
\(ga\(ga\(ga
.fi
.RE
.PP
The block and the check run under the resolved
.B \-shell
interpreter with
.BR \-c .
The default is
.BR /bin/sh .
The default prompt names the exact interpreter. Fence labels are protocol
markers and do not select another one. Use syntax that interpreter accepts;
POSIX shell syntax is the portable baseline. Its output comes back as the
next message, laid out the way a
terminal would have shown it: the command echoed with
.B $
and
.BR > ,
standard output and standard error interleaved, and the exit status when it
is not zero. A block fenced with
.BR ply ,
.BR sh ,
.BR bash ,
.B shell
or
.B zsh
is a command; an indented one is not, which is how the model quotes shell
without running it. A reply with no block at all ends the run, and that
reply is the answer.
.PP
Ply executes the action and returns its terminal result before asking the
model for another turn. If a response contains more, Ply consumes only its
first complete command block. Later blocks and text are explicitly deferred
and do not run. An empty or unfinished first block runs nothing and receives a
correction. Independent commands may be lines in one shell script; a command
that depends on earlier output belongs in the next turn.
.PP
Because the protocol is text, it needs no tool\-use API and works with any
model
.BR ask (1)
can reach.
.SS The toolbox
A tool is a program, and the toolbox is a directory of them.
.B \-t
makes
.B PATH
that directory alone, so the model reaches the programs put there and cannot
name one that was not. Build one the old way, and add to it with
.BR ln (1):
.PP
.RS
.nf
mkdir tools && ln \-s $(which ls grep sed git go) tools/
.fi
.RE
.PP
A program says what it is in the first comment after its shebang, and that
line is the catalogue the model is given.
.B ply tools
prints it. What a program does beyond that is
.BR "name \-h" ,
and beyond that, running it: the three levels of progressive disclosure,
which programs have always had.
.PP
.B \-sh
hands over every program on the machine instead. With both, the toolbox
comes first on
.BR PATH .
.PP
The toolbox aims the model; it does not sandbox it.
.BR sh (1)
has builtins, and a redirect opens a file with no program involved. The
security boundary is the process \(em its user, its container, its
.BR chroot (2)
\(em as it always was.
.SS Done
.B \-check
takes a shell command. It runs after the model stops, and the run ends only
when it exits zero; a failure goes back to the model, which keeps working.
It also runs before the first turn, so a goal already met costs nothing and
leaves no session behind. Without
.BR \-check ,
exit 0 means only that the model stopped.
.PP
When the initial check fails, its terminal transcript rides with the goal in
the first model turn and is recorded once in the Ask session. It does not
count against
.BR \-cycles ,
which counts only checks made after the model has had a chance to work.
.PP
The check is the caller's program, not the model's, so it runs with the
caller's
.B PATH
and the toolbox merely first on it. Scoping it to the toolbox would mean
.B go test ./...
needed a toolbox holding
.BR go ,
.B git
and a linker.
.PP
When the judgment needs a model rather than a program, the mechanism does
not change, because
.BR ask (1)
is a program:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ply \-sh \-check 'ask \-n \-q \-f /tmp/judge.jsonl \\
      "Does README cover install? Answer only yes or no." \\
      < README.md | grep \-qi "^yes"' "document the installer"
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B \-n \-f
is not optional there.
.BR ask (1)
continues the caller's own conversation by default, so a judge without a
thread of its own would be asked inside whatever was last discussed, once
per cycle, and would leave the answer there.
.SS The log
The conversation is an
.BR ask (1)
session: the commands are in the assistant turns and their output is in the
user turns, so the file is the whole record and
.B ask replay \-check
proves it.
.B ply
keeps no log of its own.
.PP
When
.B \-check
reaches a terminal answer, the verdict is recorded there as an
.B ask note
\(em stamped
.BR ply ,
and not folded, so it says how the run ended without changing the
conversation. Without it a session holds every command that ran and nothing
about whether the work was done, and a run that passed and a run that gave
up are the same shape on disk. It is the only thing in the file that a
program decided, and it is what
.BR hone (1)
learns from.
.PP
It is written at exactly the two points the check decides \(em passing, or
out of cycles \(em and nowhere else: a failing check the loop carries on
from is already in the conversation as the rejection the model was handed.
A run with no
.B \-check
records no verdict at all, because done is a program's opinion and there was
no program to have one.
.PP
What
.B \-s
loaded is recorded the same way, once the log exists:
.BR brief (1)
prints a skill's body without its frontmatter, so a procedure reaches the
prompt as anonymous prose and its name would otherwise live only on standard
error. A run that loaded none claims none. This is what
.B hone \-into \-
reads to put a lesson back on the procedure the run was following.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B ply \fR[\fIflags\fR] \fIgoal\fR
Work the goal. Piped standard input rides with it, or is the goal when
there is no other. Input past 64 KB is written beside the session as a
.B .stdin
file and the model is told the path, so a large log is something to grep
rather than a cost on every request. Past 16 MB is an error naming the
limit, never a truncation.
.TP
.B ply tools \fR[\fIflags\fR]
Print the catalogue the model would be given, and the
.B PATH
its commands would run with. Takes
.B \-t
and
.BR \-sh .
.TP
.B ply system \fR[\fIflags\fR] [\fIgoal\fR]
Print the system prompt that would be sent, skills included. It is a value,
not a secret, so extending it is ordinary shell rather than a config format:
.B \-S "$(ply system; cat house.md)"
\&. Takes the flags that shape it:
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-sh ,
.BR \-C ,
.BR \-check ,
.BR \-timeout ,
.B \-cap
and
.BR \-s .
A goal may follow, because
.B \-s \-
asks brief to choose a skill for one.
.TP
.B ply version
Print the version.
.B \-V
and
.B \-\-version
do the same.
.TP
.B ply help
Print the flag summary on standard output.
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-help
do the same. A usage error prints it on standard error instead.
.PP
Anything that is not a command is the goal.
.B \-\-
sends a word that is one.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-t " dir"
Toolbox:
.B PATH
becomes this directory alone. Defaults to
.BR $PLY_TOOLS .
Either this or
.B \-sh
is required; a model with no way to act is a spent call.
.TP
.B \-sh
Hand the model every program on
.BR PATH .
With
.BR \-t ,
the toolbox comes first.
.TP
.BI \-shell " executable"
Command interpreter for model blocks and checks. It must accept
.BR \-c .
Ply resolves it before calling the model and reports a missing or
non\-executable choice as an error. Defaults to
.BR $PLY_SHELL ,
or
.B /bin/sh
when that is unset. The unrelated login\-shell variable
.B $SHELL
is never consulted. This flag names one executable, not an executable plus
arguments; use a wrapper program when fixed interpreter options are required.
.TP
.BI \-check " cmd"
The goal is done when this shell command exits zero.
.TP
.B \-B
Work the goal even if the check already passes, as
.BR make (1)
takes it.
.TP
.BI \-cycles " n"
Failed checks before giving up. Default 5; 0 is unbounded.
.TP
.B \-compact
When the context window fills, carry on. A full window is permanent, so the
run would otherwise end at exit 2; with this,
.B ask compact
writes a handoff note into a fresh session and the loop moves into it,
re-sending the message the full session could not take. The note is a model
summarising the work, so it is a lossy step taken deliberately and reported
on standard error, never quietly.
.TP
.BI \-compactions " n"
Compactions before giving up. Default 3; 0 is unbounded. A goal that needs
more than a few has outgrown one run.
.TP
.BI \-turns " n"
Model turns before giving up. Default 50. Zero is explicitly unbounded.
This is the invocation-wide liveness bound: it also stops a model that keeps
writing commands and never stops for the check.
.TP
.BI \-timeout " d"
Per\-command timeout, as a Go duration such as
.BR 30s .
Default 2m. A command that outlasts it receives an interrupt by process group
so nested ply processes can stop their own command groups, then an
uncooperative group is killed after a short grace period. It reports 124,
which is
.BR timeout (1)'s
number.
.TP
.BI \-cap " n"
Bytes of a command's output kept and shown to the model, head and tail, with
the middle elided and the elision announced in the text the model reads.
Default 16384.
.TP
.BI \-C " dir"
Run commands in this directory. Default is the current one.
.TP
.BI \-m " spec"
Provider and model, passed to
.BR ask (1).
Default is ask's own
.BR $ASK_MODEL .
.TP
.BI \-S " text"
System prompt, replacing the default. The protocol lives in the default, so
.B ply system
prints what would be dropped.
.B \-S ""
sends none.
.TP
.BI \-s " name"
Append a
.BR brief (1)
skill to the system prompt. Repeats.
.B \-s \-
asks brief to choose one for the goal; brief refuses to guess, so nothing
matching is reported on standard error and the run goes on without one.
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Session log to write. Default is a new one under
.BR $PLY_DIR .
.TP
.BI \-session-out " file"
Atomically write the absolute path of the current Ask session to this file,
followed by a newline. It is written before the first model turn and replaced
after every successful compaction, so a supervising process can follow the
run without parsing the human typescript. A requested control file that
cannot be maintained is an error. It is a pointer, not a second log.
.TP
.B \-q
No typescript on standard error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PLY_TOOLS
Default toolbox directory, as
.BR \-t .
.TP
.B PLY_SHELL
Default command interpreter, as
.BR \-shell .
Ply exports the resolved absolute path to every command, so another Ply
started as a program inherits the same interpreter.
.TP
.B PLY_DIR
Where sessions live. Default
.BR ~/.ply/sessions ,
created mode 0700.
.TP
.B PLY_DEPTH
How deep this ply is inside another. It is set for every command ply runs,
incremented each time, and a ply that finds it at 8 refuses to start: a tool
that runs ply is the sub\-agent mechanism, and without a floor it is also a
fork bomb with a credit card.
.TP
.B PLY
The path to the running binary, set for every command ply runs, so a tool
can start another ply on a host with nothing else installed.
.TP
.B ASK
The
.BR ask (1)
binary to run. Default
.BR ask ,
found on
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B BRIEF
The
.BR brief (1)
binary to run, for
.BR \-s .
Default
.BR brief .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Set to any value to strip color from the typescript. Color is also stripped
when standard error is not a terminal.
.PP
Models, keys and gateways belong to
.BR ask (1).
.B ply
passes it
.BR \-m ,
.BR \-f ,
.B \-q
and the system prompt in
.BR ASK_SYSTEM ,
and nothing else.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.ply/sessions
Session logs, in
.BR ask (1)
format, mode 0600.
.TP
.B ~/.ply/sessions/*.stdin
Piped input too large to carry in the conversation.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done. The check passed, or, with no check, the model stopped.
.TP
.B 1
Error: bad usage, no
.B ask
on
.BR PATH ,
a provider failure.
.TP
.B 2
Not done: the check is still failing, a bound was reached, the command
protocol stalled, or the context window is full. It is its own status because
a supervisor has to tell "did not work" from "broke", or it retries the
wrong one.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted.
.SH EXAMPLES
Work a goal against a test suite, and only then release:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ply \-sh \-check 'go test ./...' "make the tests pass" && make release
.fi
.RE
.PP
Give the model three programs and nothing else:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mkdir tools && ln \-s $(which git rg sed) tools/
ply \-t tools \-check 'git diff \-\-quiet' "stage the obvious typo fixes"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Brief it first, then set it to work:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ply \-sh \-s web\-perf \-check './budget.sh' "get LCP under 2.5s"
ply \-sh \-s \- "make my page load faster"
.fi
.RE
.PP
As a filter, in a pipe:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kubectl logs deploy/api | ply \-t tools "find the cause of the 500s"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Prove the run afterwards:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ask replay \-check ~/.ply/sessions/20260801\-142233\-a3f9c1e0.jsonl
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ask (1),
.BR brief (1),
.BR hone (1),
.BR make (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR timeout (1)
.SH HISTORY
.B ply
is the third of four filters carved from one agent.
.BR ask (1)
took the model,
.BR brief (1)
took the procedure,
.B ply
took the loop, and
.BR hone (1)
took the lesson. Each refuses to grow the others, which is why all of them
stay small.
.PP
The four close into a circle:
.B ply
works a goal and its check decides,
.BR hone (1)
reads that verdict out of the log and writes what the run taught as a
.BR brief (1)
skill, and the next
.B ply
loads it with
.BR \-s .
.SH CAVEATS
The toolbox is not a sandbox. It scopes what the model reaches for; it does
not contain what the model does. Run
.B ply
as a user, in a container, or in a
.BR chroot (2)
you would be willing to hand a shell to, because that is what you are doing.
.PP
Without
.BR \-check ,
exit 0 is the model's opinion. That is a real answer for a goal no program
can judge, and it is a weaker one than it looks.
.PP
Changing
.B \-shell
changes parsing, builtins, startup files and sometimes the meaning of valid
syntax. Ply verifies that the named executable exists; it does not guess its
dialect or inspect a version string. The operator is responsible for choosing
an interpreter that accepts
.B \-c
and for writing a compatible check.
